<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andernach</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andernach is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a>. Founded as a Roman fort, Antunnacum, it is one of the oldest towns in Germany. Two battles were fought near it in the 9th and 10th centuries, including that of 939, in which rebels against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> were defeated and Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilbert</a> of Lotharingia drowned in the Rhine.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andernach.jpg" alt="Andernach" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maus-Trauden at German Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rheinlagen-Andernach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Andernach has about 30,000 inhabitants and lies in the district of Mayen-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a>, 21 kilometres north of Koblenz, on the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> near the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuwied">Neuwied</a> basin. A few hundred metres downstream the Rhine valley narrows, forming the northern part of the Middle Rhine stretch. In Roman times this narrow passage was called the Porta Antunnacensis, or Andernachian Gate, formed by two hills, the Krahnenberg and the Engwetter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> founded Antunnacum in 12 BCE on the site of an old Celtic settlement, probably called Antunnuac. Both names mean village or farm of Antunnos, a man not yet identified. The town held its bimillenary feast in 1988. From the 12th to the 19th century it was the southernmost outpost of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/electorate-of-cologne">Electorate of Cologne</a>. Its arms, known since 1344, pair a black cross, for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, with keys of St Peter, for the Archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, of which Andernach also formed part. The oldest town seal, made before 1200, shows St Mary as patron of its citizens. In the 19th century the town was noted for millstones, bricks and clay for tobacco pipes.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-of-tudela">Benjamin of Tudela</a> in the 12th century named Andernach among 13 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> towns with important Jewish communities. The community was periodically persecuted from the 13th to the 15th century, notably in 1348 and 1349 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a>. A new community was founded in 1860. On Kristallnacht in 1938 the synagogue was set on fire; at least 11 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from Andernach were murdered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>, and none lived there after 1945. Between 1941 and 1944 about 1,560 patients were sent from a transit hospital in the town to the Hadamar Euthanasia Centre, where they were murdered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The medieval fortifications survive in part: the Round Tower, 56 metres tall and finished in 1453, the ruins of the archiepiscopal castle with its keep, and stretches of wall with two gates. The Old Crane, a stone treadwheel crane of the 16th century, worked from 1561 to 1911 loading millstones, tuff blocks and wine casks, and is one of only a few of its kind in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to survive. The Church of Our Lady is the town's oldest historical attraction, parts of it going back to the 11th century, and a cold-water geyser in the Namedyer Werth nature reserve rises to as much as 64 m. The writer Charles Bukowski was born in Andernach, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honore-de-balzac">Honoré de Balzac</a> set his story L'Auberge rouge there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Andernach” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At <b>Andernach</b> on the Rhine, followers of King Otto I of Germany decisively defeated rebels led by Eberhard of Franconia, the brother of the late King Conrad I. The rebel leaders were killed, and Franconia came directly under the crown.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-2">2 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Near <b>Andernach</b>, the East Frankish king Louis the Younger defeated his uncle, the West Frankish king and emperor Charles the Bald, who had tried to seize the western lands of Louis's inheritance.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-8">8 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke Gilbert went over to the West Frankish king before Henry the Fowler recovered the region in 925; Gilbert drowned at <b>Andernach</b> in 939 while rebelling against Otto I. Otto's son-in-law Conrad the Red rebelled in 953.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-lotharingia">Duchy of Lotharingia</a></li></ul></section>
